1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,570
I was drawing the Sun.

3
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:10,875
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:12,505
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:15,730
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,385
At that moment, half the
world was in sunlight.

7
00:00:24,855 --> 00:00:26,245
Biscuit, no!

8
00:00:26,855 --> 00:00:28,590
Then the Sun went out.

9
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,400
MIRA: Is it night again?

10
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:37,055
It's seven in the morning.

11
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:40,535
Then someone knocked. Three times.

12
00:00:41,005 --> 00:00:44,920
MR. ITO: Good morning. Shoes
on, please. Both of you.

13
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:47,750
Mr. Ito, from upstairs.

14
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:52,635
He had a torch, a radio, and a bag
he'd packed the night before.

15
00:00:53,105 --> 00:00:56,020
MR. ITO: And bring the dog. And the toast.

16
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:58,980
It took me a year to understand why.

17
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:03,010
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

18
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:04,790
It's an eclipse!

19
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:09,770
MR. ITO: Eclipses are polite. They
leave after seven minutes.

20
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:12,655
Roof. Bring the telescope.

21
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:17,310
Kai carried it up six floors. He
complained about every one.

22
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,545
MR. ITO: Venus first.

23
00:01:20,015 --> 00:01:21,830
MIRA: Why is that one still on?

24
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,490
MR. ITO: Venus shines with
the Sun's light.

25
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:29,775
That light takes the long
way round to reach us.

26
00:01:30,245 --> 00:01:35,810
If something happened at the Sun, Venus
goes dark at four minutes past seven.

27
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:37,670
So we waited.

28
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,130
MR. ITO: Then it happened at the Sun.

29
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,165
And Jupiter will go out at
four minutes past eight.

30
00:01:49,635 --> 00:01:52,100
At four minutes past eight, it did.

31
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:56,610
He always tapped it three
times. I never asked why.

32
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,995
By nine, it was a degree colder.

33
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:13,805
(RADIO) Phone networks are down
across the city. Stay indoors.

34
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:16,435
Everyone's going to the shops.

35
00:02:16,905 --> 00:02:19,145
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

36
00:02:19,615 --> 00:02:23,080
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

37
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:26,065
Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

38
00:02:26,535 --> 00:02:27,975
MIRA: They're on my list.

39
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:31,375
Two grown men fought over the last heater.

40
00:02:31,845 --> 00:02:35,310
MR. ITO: Heaters need
electricity. They'll learn.

41
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:39,845
(RADIO) Now, messages from
hospital staff to their families.

42
00:02:40,515 --> 00:02:45,455
Kai, Mira, it's Mum. They need me at
the hospital. Stay with Mr. Ito.

43
00:02:45,925 --> 00:02:49,010
Mum was a nurse. She'd been
on the night shift.

44
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:51,245
Is the Sun… dead?

45
00:02:52,015 --> 00:02:55,555
MR. ITO: The Moon pulls the
sea. So does the Sun.

46
00:02:56,025 --> 00:03:00,540
If the Sun were gone, today's tide
would be a third smaller.

47
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:02,675
Go and look.

48
00:03:03,145 --> 00:03:05,835
So, Kai and I walked down to the harbour.

49
00:03:06,305 --> 00:03:08,265
MIRA: It's exactly on the line!

50
00:03:08,735 --> 00:03:10,695
So the Sun's still there.

51
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:12,575
MIRA: Then where is it?

52
00:03:13,345 --> 00:03:17,205
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

53
00:03:18,175 --> 00:03:21,340
MIRA: The sea's growing a
skin. The fish don't mind!

54
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:25,195
On Day 3, the sky did something beautiful.

55
00:03:25,665 --> 00:03:30,980
(RADIO) …a solar storm that left the Sun
four days ago, still arriving tonight.

56
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:33,115
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

57
00:03:40,785 --> 00:03:43,495
On Day 7, the power went out.

58
00:03:43,965 --> 00:03:47,280
MR. ITO: Fill the bath.
Before the pipes freeze.

59
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:51,160
Mr. Ito showed us how to
make one warm room.

60
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:54,215
Free fridge. Finally, some good news.

61
00:03:54,685 --> 00:03:57,670
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

62
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,555
He was holding my hand too hard.

63
00:04:01,525 --> 00:04:03,265
Then we smelled smoke.

64
00:04:03,735 --> 00:04:06,950
MR. ITO: They're burning
furniture. Indoors.

65
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:11,860
Everyone out. The smoke you can
see. The gas you can't.

66
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:14,915
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

67
00:04:15,385 --> 00:04:17,945
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

68
00:04:18,415 --> 00:04:19,550
MIRA: BISCUIT!

69
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:22,685
Biscuit! Come on, boy!

70
00:04:23,155 --> 00:04:27,295
Kai searched all night. Then
again, it was always night now.

71
00:04:29,965 --> 00:04:32,050
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

72
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:35,505
They led to the only warm
thing on the street.

73
00:04:35,975 --> 00:04:38,140
You stupid, brilliant dog.

74
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:42,670
A few metres down, the ground
stays around ten degrees.

75
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:46,700
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

76
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:50,755
By morning, half the street had
followed him underground.

77
00:04:51,225 --> 00:04:55,010
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

78
00:04:55,480 --> 00:05:00,215
We grew food under pink lamps.
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

79
00:05:00,685 --> 00:05:05,070
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

80
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:07,680
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

81
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:11,810
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

82
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:15,565
Some of them had already
forgotten what colour it was.

83
00:05:16,635 --> 00:05:19,800
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

84
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:31,235
Mr. Ito spent weeks rebuilding
a firefighter's camera.

85
00:05:31,705 --> 00:05:37,145
MR. ITO: It sees heat, not light. If
something's out there, it has to be warm.

86
00:05:37,615 --> 00:05:40,850
On Day 40, he took us up to the surface.

87
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,955
He pointed it where the Sun
should have been.

88
00:05:44,425 --> 00:05:45,940
MR. ITO: It's warm.

89
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:50,300
And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

90
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:52,905
There was something around the Sun.

91
00:05:53,375 --> 00:05:55,690
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

92
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,245
MIRA: Who built it?

93
00:05:59,115 --> 00:06:01,625
Nobody could build that in a day.

94
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:04,210
MR. ITO: No. Nobody did.

95
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:08,345
Nineteen-nineteen. Look at the lines.

96
00:06:08,815 --> 00:06:13,380
It was always there. Edge-on,
like the slats of a blind.

97
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:17,990
And on the morning the Sun went
out, someone closed it.

98
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,950
MIRA: You've seen those lines before?

99
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:26,135
MR. ITO: I saw them once. A long
time ago. Nobody listened.

100
00:06:27,005 --> 00:06:29,645
I believed him. It was even true.

101
00:06:30,715 --> 00:06:34,080
On Day 100, the generators failed.

102
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,915
MR. ITO: I know a little
about old machines.

103
00:06:38,385 --> 00:06:40,345
That was the coldest night.

104
00:06:40,815 --> 00:06:42,880
How does this end, Mira?

105
00:06:44,150 --> 00:06:46,165
MIRA: With the lights coming back on.

106
00:06:47,235 --> 00:06:49,750
On Day 180, they did.

107
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:51,835
And so did Mum.

108
00:06:53,105 --> 00:06:54,995
You kept them alive.

109
00:06:55,465 --> 00:06:57,455
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

110
00:06:58,325 --> 00:06:59,785
MIRA: What are you building?

111
00:07:00,325 --> 00:07:02,540
MR. ITO: Something to listen with.

112
00:07:03,410 --> 00:07:07,595
By Day 300, his camera had
a much bigger lens.

113
00:07:08,065 --> 00:07:10,925
A thin, warm thread was leaving the shell.

114
00:07:11,395 --> 00:07:15,860
MR. ITO: It's sending the light away.
Out of the plane of the planets.

115
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:17,845
Sending it where?

116
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:22,570
MR. ITO: Twenty-two.

117
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:28,180
He whispered a number. Twenty-two.
I didn't ask what it meant.

118
00:07:28,650 --> 00:07:31,490
All the Sun's light, sent to one star.

119
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,875
On Day 365, we all went up to
the surface together.

120
00:07:42,345 --> 00:07:45,110
MR. ITO: I need to point this at the Sun.

121
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:49,094
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

122
00:07:49,564 --> 00:07:51,779
MR. ITO: It's coming from the Sun.

123
00:07:52,249 --> 00:07:53,034
One.

124
00:07:53,784 --> 00:07:54,319
Two.

125
00:07:55,069 --> 00:07:55,804
Three.

126
00:07:56,974 --> 00:07:59,039
Mercury. Venus. Earth.

127
00:07:59,509 --> 00:08:01,249
It stopped at three.

128
00:08:02,519 --> 00:08:03,727
MIRA: That's us.

129
00:08:05,197 --> 00:08:06,162
MR. ITO: No.

130
00:08:07,112 --> 00:08:09,652
It isn't counting planets.

131
00:08:24,122 --> 00:08:29,037
The man in the observatory was Mr.
Ito. Forty-four years ago.

132
00:08:29,907 --> 00:08:33,122
He found the lines, and the
star they pointed to.

133
00:08:33,592 --> 00:08:36,182
And he knocked. One. Two. Three.

134
00:08:37,052 --> 00:08:41,167
Twenty-two years to get there.
Twenty-two years to come back.

135
00:08:41,637 --> 00:08:45,852
MR. ITO: It's been knocking back since
the morning the Sun went out.

136
00:08:46,322 --> 00:08:50,037
I was the only one who
knew where to listen.

137
00:08:50,507 --> 00:08:52,547
It isn't counting.

138
00:08:53,197 --> 00:08:54,962
It's answering.

139
00:08:56,232 --> 00:08:58,397
I'm sorry, Mira.

140
00:09:00,067 --> 00:09:02,957
That night, Mr. Ito told the world.

141
00:09:03,427 --> 00:09:05,687
Every radio on Earth heard it.

142
00:09:08,157 --> 00:09:12,992
Every morning for forty-four years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

143
00:09:14,062 --> 00:09:17,097
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

144
00:09:18,967 --> 00:09:21,407
Whoever answered is still out there.

145
00:09:21,757 --> 00:09:24,742
What would you say back? Tell
us in the comments.
